WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:03.005
Every team at Cymbal wants a database.

2
00:00:03.025 --> 00:00:07.440
The catalog, the carts, price history, sessions, and product images.

3
00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:09.015
There is no one best store.

4
00:00:09.035 --> 00:00:12.265
Pick by how the data is read and written.

5
00:00:12.285 --> 00:00:14.700
Ask four questions, in this order.

6
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:16.450
Analytics or transactions?

7
00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:17.787
Relational or not?

8
00:00:17.807 --> 00:00:19.175
How big, and how global?

9
00:00:19.195 --> 00:00:20.635
And how fast?

10
00:00:20.655 --> 00:00:22.860
Analytics over huge data, with SQL?

11
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.570
That is BigQuery.

12
00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:27.805
Files, images and backups go to Cloud Storage.

13
00:00:27.825 --> 00:00:30.142
A normal relational app, in one region?

14
00:00:30.162 --> 00:00:31.690
Cloud SQL.

15
00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.552
Relational, but global, with strong consistency?

16
00:00:34.572 --> 00:00:35.775
Spanner.

17
00:00:35.795 --> 00:00:39.213
Huge key-value data, with very fast writes, like sensors?

18
00:00:39.233 --> 00:00:40.485
Bigtable.

19
00:00:40.505 --> 00:00:41.947
Documents for a mobile app?

20
00:00:41.967 --> 00:00:42.660
Firestore.

21
00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:43.348
A cache?

22
00:00:43.368 --> 00:00:44.770
Memorystore.

23
00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:50.370
The trap: BigQuery as an app database, for single row lookups and updates.

24
00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:53.605
Bigtable has one index only: the row key.

25
00:00:53.625 --> 00:00:57.515
Rows are stored sorted by key, like words in a dictionary.

26
00:00:57.535 --> 00:01:01.552
So if every key starts with the time, all new writes land on one server.

27
00:01:01.572 --> 00:01:02.975
A hotspot.

28
00:01:02.995 --> 00:01:06.638
The fix: start the key with something that spreads, like the sensor ID.

29
00:01:06.658 --> 00:01:08.135
Then the time.

30
00:01:08.155 --> 00:01:11.835
And design the key around your most common query.

31
00:01:11.855 --> 00:01:15.095
Next, the relational family, in one line each.

32
00:01:15.115 --> 00:01:17.283
Cloud SQL scales up, not out.

33
00:01:17.303 --> 00:01:20.505
High availability adds a standby in another zone.

34
00:01:20.525 --> 00:01:24.540
AlloyDB is PostgreSQL that is also fast for analytics.

35
00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:30.750
Spanner scales out across the world, with 99.999 percent availability.

36
00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:32.488
Memorystore is a cache.

37
00:01:32.508 --> 00:01:35.225
Never make it your system of record.

38
00:01:35.245 --> 00:01:36.437
Now Cloud Storage.

39
00:01:36.457 --> 00:01:38.985
Four classes, by how often you read.

40
00:01:39.005 --> 00:01:40.298
Standard for hot data.

41
00:01:40.318 --> 00:01:41.623
Nearline for monthly.

42
00:01:41.643 --> 00:01:43.035
Coldline for quarterly.

43
00:01:43.055 --> 00:01:44.845
Archive for yearly.

44
00:01:44.865 --> 00:01:46.182
Here is the surprise.

45
00:01:46.202 --> 00:01:49.180
All four are equally fast and equally safe.

46
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.365
Colder classes are cheaper to store, but you pay to read, and for deleting early.

47
00:01:54.385 --> 00:01:56.753
Lifecycle rules move objects for you.

48
00:01:56.773 --> 00:01:59.675
Autoclass does it when you do not know the pattern.

49
00:01:59.695 --> 00:02:00.750
For compliance,

50
00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:05.925
a retention policy with Bucket Lock makes objects impossible to delete.

51
00:02:05.945 --> 00:02:08.185
Last: let time save you money.

52
00:02:08.205 --> 00:02:13.220
In BigQuery, a table you have not changed for 90 days gets cheaper by itself.

53
00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:17.680
So do not export old tables to Cloud Storage just to save money.

54
00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.380
And set an expiration on data you do not need.

55
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.529
It deletes itself.

56
00:02:22.549 --> 00:02:23.789
Recap.

57
00:02:23.809 --> 00:02:25.464
Four questions pick the store.

58
00:02:25.484 --> 00:02:28.249
The row key is Bigtable's only index.

59
00:02:28.269 --> 00:02:30.849
Storage classes share speed, not price.

60
00:02:30.869 --> 00:02:34.059
And old BigQuery data gets cheaper by itself.

61
00:02:34.079 --> 00:02:36.854
Try the checkpoint below.
